    "fullText": "Trace Minerals/Micro-Trace Minerals Low blood levels of Ni are observed in people with vitamin B6 deficiency, cirrhosis of the liver, and kidney failure.  The significance of these blood levels is not known.  In contrast, elevated blood levels of nickel are associated with the development of cancer, heart attack, thyroid disorders, psoriasis and eczema.  Deficiency: Nickel settles in sinus, joints, and spinal column.  Can be a nephro-toxin, effecting the urinary tract, especially the kidneys.  It is found to bind with blood fungus causing tumors.  Can paralyze the spinal column and bring on epilepsy.  Can cause dermatitis and other skin conditions, allergic reactions and chronic rhinitis.  Inflammation of lungs and liver, leading to necrosis and carcinoma.  Toxicity: Leads to paralysis, overflow of blood to brain, and epilepsy.  In excess, can be a carcinogenic.  Can rob the body of oxygen.  Every tumor needs nickel to hold it together!"
