    "fullText": "Basic Tissue Salts The following are the basic twelve tissue or cell salts set forth in W. H.  Schussler’s biochemical theories of the 1870s.  Chloride of potash Other names: Kali muriaticum, kali.  mur. , potassium chloride Found in these tissues: Fibrin, unites with hydrogen to form HCL (hydrochloric acid); aids in the production and promotion of bile; alkalizer; aids in digestive enzyme formation.  Deficiency: Digestive problems, excessive thinness, weakened tissues, congestive and excessive mucus production, swellings, granulation of eyelids, nose, etc. , sluggish liver, and jaundice.  Chloride of soda Other names: Natrum muriaticum, nat.  mur. , sodium chloride Found in these tissues: Found in all tissues and fluids of the body, especially extracellular.  An alkalizer found in gastric juices.  Deficiency: Fluid regulation, acidosis, dehydration, elevated carbon dioxide and cellular carbonic acid, constipation, heat stroke, ulcers, heart palpitations."
