    "excerpt": "Jonathan Weiner, in his Pulitzer-prize winning book The Beak Of The Finch, describes researchers Peter and Rosemary Grant's observations that different finch \"species\" do breed together and produce fertile offspring.  The second type involves chance mutations where the chromosomes suddenly double (as in plants), or cha...",
    "fullText": "O R I G I N S Jonathan Weiner, in his Pulitzer-prize winning book The Beak Of The Finch, describes researchers Peter and Rosemary Grant's observations that different finch \"species\" do breed together and produce fertile offspring.  The second type involves chance mutations where the chromosomes suddenly double (as in plants), or change in some other way, but these mutations have never been shown to reproduce themselves into a new species.  The fact that a \"species\" cannot be precisely defined disassembles the entire darwinian classification system which relies on categorization.  The Fallacy Of Radio-Isotope Dating The vertebrate sequence A reflection in rock Obscured by the ages, A frozen time clock.  But how many years, Into the din, Dates each fossil We're examining?  More than anything else, evolutionists do not like having their dating system challenged."
