    "excerpt": "Green-leafy vegetation is also an excellent detoxifier of the liver, especially wild green vegetation.  The calcium, magnesium and iron in deep green and wild green vegetation bind with heavy metals, chemicals and chemical drugs and allow the body to wash them out as salts through the urine.  If green-leafed vegetables...",
    "fullText": "T H E Secrft Revealed Green-leafy vegetation is also an excellent detoxifier of the liver, especially wild green vegetation.  The calcium, magnesium and iron in deep green and wild green vegetation bind with heavy metals, chemicals and chemical drugs and allow the body to wash them out as salts through the urine.  If green-leafed vegetables, which are high in oxalic acid, such as beet greens, chard, lamb's quarters, rhubarb, and/or spinach are cooked, then the heat- altered oxalic acid can combine with calcium in the body, interfere with iron absorption, and eventually (if eaten in large quantities over a long period of time) form stones in the kidneys.  If eaten raw, the oxalates in these vegetables are normally metabolized properly by the body.  If one feels kidney pains 3-6 hours after eating these vegetables (even if in the raw state), then they should be avoided.  Alkaloids Alkaloids are bitter organic compounds which are derived from plants bearing seed."
