    "fullText": "17. Bobinsana (*Calliandra angustifolia*) **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : Thus far, only one laboratory study has been published on bobinsana.  Researchers in Sweden evaluated the anti-inflammatory action of a ethanol extract of the tree's bark.  While they reported that it was inactive with a topical application on rat's ears, they did report that the extract inhibited COX-1 prostaglandin biosynthesis.  COX-inhibitors are a class of pharmaceutical drugs for arthritis and ...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : In the Amazon, the Indians of the Rio Pastaza consider bobinsana to be a stimulant.  They prepare a decoction of the roots to take for strength and energy.  The Shipibo-Conibo Indians in the Ucayali area of Peru call the tree semein and prepare a bark tincture for rheumatism, arthritis, colds, uterine disorders, and edema (or water retention).  The in..."
