    "fullText": "44. Copaiba (*Copaifera officinalis*) **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : Much of the clinical research performed to date has verified the traditional uses of copaiba.  One of copaiba's main uses in herbal medicine is to help heal wounds.  In 2002, researchers in Brazil first reported that it was highly effective as a topical wound healer in animal studies.  Since then, six more in vivo animal studies have been published in 2009, 2010, 2013, and 2017 which confirm that usi...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : On the Rio Solimoes in northwest Amazonia, copaiba resin is used topically by indigenous tribes as a wound healer, to stop bleeding, for skin sores and psoriasis, and to treat gonorrhea.  Healers and curanderos in the Amazon today use copaiba resin for all types of pain, for skin disorders and insect bites, and to cool inflammation.  In Brazilian her..."
