    "fullText": "54. Epazote (*Chenopodium ambrosioides*) htm) - **Principes Phytochimiques Actifs** :  - Epazote is rich in chemicals called monoterpenes  - In other in vitro clinical studies  - The main chemicals found in epazote include α-pinene  - α-terpinene  - α-terpineol aritasone  - ascaridole  - β-sitosterol  - benzyl beta-D-glucopyranoside - **Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques** :  - Not only does the plant have toxic activity  - it has also been traditionally used to induce abortions  - However - **Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor)** : 1.  **Actions Principales (Rain-Tree)** : 1.  The plant and essential oil should not be used during pregnancy and lactation.  Not only does the plant have toxic activity, it has also been traditionally used to induce abortions.  2.  While epazote has been used by indigenous tribes as a contraceptive, this use is not verified by clinical research (nor should it be relied on for such).  However, the use of the plant is probably contraindicated for couples trying to get pregnant.  3."
