    "fullText": "54. Epazote (*Chenopodium ambrosioides*) The oil of epazote is considered extremely toxic and should not be taken internally.  2.  **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : A decoction and infusion of the plant was analyzed in vitro to determine if they had toxic effects.  At various concentrations the extracts caused cellular aberrations in the test tube, indicating possible toxic effects.  However, in the 1970's the World Health Organization reported that a decoction of 20 g of leaves rapidly expelled parasites without any apparent side effects in humans.  In 1996 ext...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : In the Yucatan, indigenous Indian groups have long used epazote for intestinal parasites, asthma, excessive mucus, chorea (a type of rheumatic fever that affects the brain) and other nervous afflictions.  The Tikuna Indians in the Amazon use it to expel intestinal worms and as a mild laxative.  The Siona-Secoya and Kofan Indian tribes in South Americ..."
