    "fullText": "73. Jaborandi (*Pilocarpus jaborandi*) htm) - **Principes Phytochimiques Actifs** :  - to folklore use  - and then into modern medicine based upon natural chemicals found in the plant  - In 1875  - In 1876  - The mixture of pilocarpine and another natural product  - physostigmine  - remains to this day one of the mainstay drugs in ophthalmology  - Interestingly - **Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques** :  - An overdose may cause such symptoms as flushing  - profuse sweating and salivation  - urinary frequency  - nausea  - rapid pulse  - contracted pupils  - diarrhea or fatal pulmonary edema  - The plant may induce bradycardia - **Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor)** : 1.  **Actions Principales (Rain-Tree)** : 1.  Pilocarpine has shown to increase the rate of birth defects in animal studies.  Jaborandi should not be taken during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.  2.  Both jaborandi and pilocarpine may cause headaches and can irritate the stomach and cause vomiting and nausea."
