    "fullText": "78. Jergon Sacha (*Dracontium loretense*) **Actions Principales (Rain-Tree)** : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs.  2.  **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : Despite the large and growing market for jergón sacha, not a single clinical study has been published on its actions.  If jergón sacha's longstanding use as an effective snakebite remedy was clinically validated, it may explain its more recent use as an antiviral for HIV as well.  The most recent class of drugs developed for HIV are called protease inhibitors .  Protease inhibitors work by blocking a...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : Ethnobotanically, jergón sacha is considered a \"signature plant\": the plant's indigenous uses are directly related to its appearance.  In this particular case, the trunk-like stem and its mottled coloring closely resembles a poisonous snake indigenous to the areas in which it grows.  In Peru and Ecuador, the name of both snake and plant is jergón sac..."
