    "fullText": "84. Manaca (*Brunfelsia uniflorus*) Those taking blood-thinning medications such as coumadin should use manacá only under the direction and supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner to monitor these effects.  5.  The plant chemical scopoletin has been documented to inhibit monoamine oxidase.  Those taking monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors should consult their healthcare practitioner before taking manacá.  2.  **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : Much more research is published on various chemicals found in manacá, rather than the plant itself.  However, several animal studies do confirm some of its traditional uses - especially for pain and inflammation.  In a 1991 clinical study with mice, manacá demonstrated pain-relieving and anti-inflammatory effects.  An earlier (1977) study reported that manacá root extracts evidenced marked anti-infla...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : Manacá has a long history of indigenous use for both medicine and magic."
