    "fullText": "84. Manaca (*Brunfelsia uniflorus*) Its Brazilian common name, manacá , originated with the Tupi Indians in Brazil; they named it after the most beautiful girl in their tribe, Manacán, for its lovely flowers.  It is a sacred and spiritual plant used by shamans and curanderos in the potion ayahuasca (a sacred hall...  - **Posologie & Modes d'Administration** :  - **Dosage Standard** : relieves pain prevents mutations Root reduces fever kills insects Decoction: 1/2 cup twice daily reduces inflammation promotes perspiration Capsules: 1 g twice daily detoxifies blood lowers body temperature Tincture: 1-2 ml twice daily depresses CNS moderately sedative increases urination mildly laxative moves lymphatic fluid promotes menstruation  - **Infusion / Décoction** : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.   - **Teinture** : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour.  - **Contre-indications & Précautions** :  - 1.  Manacá has a traditional use as an abortive."
