    "fullText": "104. Quinine Bark (*Cinchona officinalis*) Malaria strains have evolved which have developed a resistance to the synthesized quinine drugs.  It was shown in early studies that an effective dose of natural quinine bark extract elicited the same antimalarial ac...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : Cinchona, or quinine bark, is one of the rainforest's most famous plants and most important discoveries.  Legend has it that the name cinchona came from the countess of Chinchon, the wife of a Peruvian viceroy, who was cured of a malarial type of fever by using the bark of the cinchona tree in 1638.  It was supposedly introduced to European medicine ..."
