    "fullText": "113. Simarouba (*Simarouba amara*) htm) - **Principes Phytochimiques Actifs** :  - The main active group of chemicals in simarouba are called quassinoids  - which belong to the triterpene chemical family  - Quassinoids are found in many plants and are well known to scientists  - Several of the quassinoids found in simarouba  - such as ailanthinone  - glaucarubinone  - and holacanthone  - anti-amebic - **Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques** :  - When France suffered a dysentery epidemic from 1718 to 1725  - simarouba bark was one of the few effective treatments  - malaria  - and dysentery  - as a hemostatic agent to stop bleeding  - and as a tonic - **Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor)** : 1.  **Actions Principales (Rain-Tree)** : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs.  2.  **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : After a 200-year documented history of use for dysentery around the world, its use for amebic dysentery was finally validated by conventional doctors in 1918."
