    "fullText": "114. Stevia (*Stevia rebaudiana*) htm) - **Principes Phytochimiques Actifs** :  - Western interest in stevia began around the turn of the nineteenth century  - It was first studied in 1899 by Paraguayan botanist Moises S  - Bertoni  - who wrote some of the earliest articles on stevia (in the early 1900s)  - Over 100 phytochemicals have been discovered in stevia since  - It is rich in terpenes and flavonoids  - The constituents responsible for stevia's sweetness were documented in 1931  - when eight novel plant chemicals called glycosides were discovered and named - **Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques** :  - Stevia leaf has been documented to have a hypotensive - **Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor)** : 1.  **Actions Principales (Rain-Tree)** : 1.  Stevia leaf (at dosages higher than used for sweetening purposes) has been documented to have a hypoglycemic effect.  Those with diabetes should use high amounts of stevia with caution and monitor their blood sugar levels as medications may need adjusting.  2."
