    "excerpt": "This information is not intended to be used to diagnose, prescribe or replace proper medical care.  The plant described herein is not intended to treat, cure, diagnose, mitigate or prevent any disease.  Please refer to our Conditions of Use for using this plant database file and web site.  2.  **Recherche Clinique & Ac...",
    "fullText": "117. Ubos (*Spondias mombin*) This information is not intended to be used to diagnose, prescribe or replace proper medical care.  The plant described herein is not intended to treat, cure, diagnose, mitigate or prevent any disease.  Please refer to our Conditions of Use for using this plant database file and web site.  2.  **Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique** : Toxicity studies with mice and rats were performed on ubos in several published studies.  The leaves were reported non-toxic in animals when administered orally (up to 5 g per kg of an animal's weight).  Toxicity was noted however when animals were injected with a leaf extract (LD50 was between 1. 36 and 1. 86 gm / kg).  The use of ubos leaves by midwives has been studied and reported over several year...  3.  **Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel** : Ubos is widely relied on for various herbal remedies for numerous conditions and virtually every part of the tree is used — from its thick corky bark, to its leaves, fruits, roots, to even its flowers."
